// Class 12 Chemistry practice bank, part 2: Haloalkanes and Haloarenes, Alcohols/Phenols/Ethers, Aldehydes/Ketones/Carboxylic Acids, Amines, Biomolecules.
StudyData.addPractice(12, "chemistry", {
  "haloalkanes-and-haloarenes": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The conversion of CH₃CH₂Br into CH₃CH₂I using NaI in dry acetone is known as the:", options: ["Swarts reaction", "Sandmeyer reaction", "Finkelstein reaction", "Wurtz reaction"], answer: 2, explain: "Finkelstein reaction: R–Cl/R–Br + NaI (dry acetone) → R–I. NaCl or NaBr is insoluble in acetone and precipitates, driving the reaction forward. Swarts makes alkyl fluorides.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which reagent is preferred for converting ethanol into chloroethane because both by-products escape as gases?", options: ["SOCl₂", "PCl₅", "PCl₃", "HCl with anhydrous ZnCl₂"], answer: 0, explain: "CH₃CH₂OH + SOCl₂ → CH₃CH₂Cl + SO₂↑ + HCl↑. PCl₅ and PCl₃ leave POCl₃ or H₃PO₃, which must be separated.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a vinylic halide?", options: ["CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl", "CH₂=CHCH₂Cl", "C₆H₅CH₂Cl", "CH₂=CHCl"], answer: 3, explain: "In a vinylic halide the halogen is on an sp² carbon of a C=C bond: CH₂=CHCl. CH₂=CHCH₂Cl is allylic and C₆H₅CH₂Cl is benzylic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Among the isomers below, which has the highest boiling point?", options: ["CH₃CH₂CH₂Br", "CH₃CH₂CH₂I", "CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl", "CH₃CH₂CH₂F"], answer: 1, explain: "For the same alkyl group, boiling point rises with the size and mass of the halogen: RI > RBr > RCl > RF, because of stronger van der Waals forces.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The carbon atom bonded to chlorine in chlorobenzene is:", options: ["sp³ hybridised", "sp hybridised", "dsp² hybridised", "sp² hybridised"], answer: 3, explain: "Every ring carbon of benzene is sp² hybridised, including the one bearing Cl. This makes the C–Cl bond shorter and stronger than in an alkyl chloride.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Which alkyl bromide reacts fastest with water by the SN1 mechanism?", options: ["(CH₃)₃CBr", "CH₃CH₂Br", "(CH₃)₂CHBr", "CH₃Br"], answer: 0, explain: "SN1 rate depends on the stability of the carbocation formed. The tertiary carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺ is the most stable (+I effect and hyperconjugation), so (CH₃)₃CBr is fastest.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The major product when propene reacts with HBr in the absence of peroxide is:", options: ["1-bromopropane", "1,2-dibromopropane", "2-bromopropane", "allyl bromide"], answer: 2, explain: "Markovnikov's rule: H adds to the carbon already holding more hydrogens, so Br goes to C-2, giving CH₃CHBrCH₃ via the more stable secondary carbocation.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Nitration of chlorobenzene with conc. HNO₃ and conc. H₂SO₄ gives mainly:", options: ["m-nitrochlorobenzene", "only o-nitrochlorobenzene", "a mixture of o- and p-nitrochlorobenzene, with p- as the major product", "3,5-dinitrochlorobenzene"], answer: 2, explain: "Chlorine deactivates the ring by its −I effect but directs incoming groups to ortho and para positions by its +R effect. The para isomer predominates because of less steric hindrance.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Chlorobenzene is less reactive than chloroethane towards nucleophilic substitution.\nReason (R): In chlorobenzene the C–Cl bond has partial double-bond character because of resonance.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The lone pair on Cl delocalises into the ring, so the C–Cl bond is shorter, stronger and harder to break. This is the main reason haloarenes resist substitution.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a laboratory, optically pure (R)-2-bromobutane is stirred with NaI in dry acetone. The rate is found to depend on the concentration of both the bromide and iodide. The product is:", options: ["(R)-2-iodobutane", "(S)-2-iodobutane", "a racemic mixture of 2-iodobutane", "1-iodobutane"], answer: 1, explain: "A rate law first order in both reactants means SN2, which proceeds with inversion of configuration. I and Br both have the highest CIP priority, so the spatial inversion changes the descriptor from R to S.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which of the following gives a white precipitate immediately when shaken with alcoholic AgNO₃ at room temperature?", options: ["(CH₃)₃CCl", "C₆H₅Cl", "CH₂=CHCl", "CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl"], answer: 0, explain: "The tertiary chloride ionises readily (SN1) to give Cl⁻ and a stable carbocation, so AgCl forms at once. Chlorobenzene and vinyl chloride do not ionise, and the primary chloride reacts only slowly on warming.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): (CH₃)₃C–Cl reacts with aqueous NaOH faster by the SN2 mechanism than CH₃Cl does.\nReason (R): SN2 reactions are slowed down by steric hindrance around the carbon bearing the halogen.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Backside attack is blocked by the three methyl groups in the tertiary halide, so its SN2 rate is very low; CH₃Cl is fastest in SN2. A is false, R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which sequence converts 1-bromopropane into 2-bromopropane?", options: ["Aqueous KOH, then PBr₃", "Alcoholic KOH, then HBr in the presence of peroxide", "Alcoholic KOH, then HBr (no peroxide)", "NaI in dry acetone"], answer: 2, explain: "CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + alc. KOH → CH₃CH=CH₂ (dehydrohalogenation). Then HBr adds by Markovnikov's rule to give CH₃CHBrCH₃. With peroxide the anti-Markovnikov product (1-bromopropane) would return.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: Chloroform was once used as an anaesthetic. In air and light it slowly oxidises: 2CHCl₃ + O₂ → 2COCl₂ + 2HCl. Hospitals therefore store it in dark, completely filled bottles with about 1% ethanol. If 119.5 g of CHCl₃ (M = 119.5 g mol⁻¹) were fully oxidised, the mass of phosgene (COCl₂, M = 99 g mol⁻¹) formed would be:", options: ["49.5 g", "198 g", "119.5 g", "99 g"], answer: 3, explain: "119.5 g is 1 mol of CHCl₃, which gives 1 mol of COCl₂ (mole ratio 2:2), i.e. 99 g. The ethanol converts any phosgene formed into harmless diethyl carbonate.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(Cl)CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₃.", a: "2-Chloro-4-methylpentane (the longest chain has five carbons; numbering gives the lowest locants 2 and 4).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the reaction and a suitable reagent for converting bromoethane into fluoroethane.", a: "Swarts reaction: CH₃CH₂Br + AgF (or Hg₂F₂, CoF₂, SbF₃) → CH₃CH₂F + AgBr.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Haloalkanes react with KCN to give alkyl cyanides but with AgCN to give isocyanides as the main product. Explain.", a: "KCN is ionic and provides free CN⁻ ions. The carbon end attacks the alkyl halide (the C–C bond formed is more stable), giving R–CN.\nAgCN is covalent: the carbon is bonded to silver, so only the lone pair on nitrogen is available for attack, giving R–N≡C (isocyanide).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How will you distinguish chemically between chlorobenzene and benzyl chloride (C₆H₅CH₂Cl)?", a: "Warm each with aqueous NaOH, acidify with dilute HNO₃ and add AgNO₃ solution.\nBenzyl chloride is hydrolysed (C₆H₅CH₂Cl + NaOH → C₆H₅CH₂OH + NaCl), so Cl⁻ gives a white precipitate of AgCl. Chlorobenzene is not hydrolysed under these conditions and gives no precipitate.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write the equations (with conditions) for: (i) Wurtz reaction of bromoethane, (ii) Wurtz–Fittig reaction of bromobenzene with bromomethane, (iii) Fittig reaction of bromobenzene.", a: "(i) 2CH₃CH₂Br + 2Na → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ + 2NaBr (dry ether).\n(ii) C₆H₅Br + CH₃Br + 2Na → C₆H₅CH₃ + 2NaBr (dry ether).\n(iii) 2C₆H₅Br + 2Na → C₆H₅–C₆H₅ (biphenyl) + 2NaBr (dry ether).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the SN2 mechanism of the hydrolysis of CH₃Br by OH⁻. Mention the rate law, the transition state and the stereochemical result.", a: "CH₃Br + OH⁻ → CH₃OH + Br⁻ occurs in a single step.\nThe nucleophile OH⁻ attacks the carbon from the side opposite to Br (backside attack); a pentacoordinate transition state [HO···CH₃···Br]⁻ forms in which the C–O bond is partly formed and the C–Br bond partly broken.\nRate = k[CH₃Br][OH⁻] (second order). If the carbon is a stereocentre, the configuration is inverted (Walden inversion).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the major organic product in each case: (i) CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + alcoholic KOH, heat; (ii) 2-bromobutane + alcoholic KOH, heat; (iii) CH₃CH₂Br + Mg in dry ether.", a: "(i) CH₃CH=CH₂ (propene) by β-elimination of HBr.\n(ii) But-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, as the major product (Saytzeff rule: the more substituted alkene predominates) with some but-1-ene.\n(iii) CH₃CH₂MgBr (ethylmagnesium bromide, a Grignard reagent).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Haloarenes are much less reactive than haloalkanes towards nucleophilic substitution. (a) Give four reasons. (b) How does the presence of nitro groups at ortho/para positions change the reactivity? Give the conditions for converting chlorobenzene and 2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene into the corresponding phenols.", a: "(a) (1) Resonance: the lone pair on Cl delocalises into the ring, so the C–Cl bond has partial double-bond character and is hard to break.\n(2) The carbon is sp² hybridised (more s-character), so the C–Cl bond is shorter and stronger.\n(3) The phenyl cation that SN1 would need is unstable (no resonance stabilisation of the empty sp² orbital).\n(4) The electron-rich π-cloud repels the incoming nucleophile.\n(b) Electron-withdrawing –NO₂ groups at o/p positions stabilise the negatively charged intermediate and make substitution easier.\nChlorobenzene → phenol: aq. NaOH, 623 K, 300 atm, then H⁺ (sodium phenoxide first). 2,4-Dinitrochlorobenzene → 2,4-dinitrophenol: aq. NaOH at 368 K, then H⁺.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Compare the SN1 and SN2 mechanisms with respect to (i) number of steps, (ii) rate law, (iii) order of reactivity of substrates, (iv) stereochemistry at a chiral carbon, (v) a favourable type of solvent. Give one example of each.", a: "(i) SN1: two steps (ionisation to a carbocation, then attack by the nucleophile). SN2: one concerted step with a pentacoordinate transition state.\n(ii) SN1: rate = k[RX] (first order). SN2: rate = k[RX][Nu⁻] (second order).\n(iii) SN1: 3° > 2° > 1° > CH₃X (stable carbocation). SN2: CH₃X > 1° > 2° > 3° (steric hindrance).\n(iv) SN1: racemisation (planar carbocation attacked from either face). SN2: complete inversion of configuration.\n(v) SN1: polar protic solvents (water, alcohols) stabilise the ions. SN2: polar aprotic solvents (acetone, DMF).\nExamples: (CH₃)₃CBr + H₂O → (CH₃)₃COH (SN1); CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH (SN2).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A student has three isomeric bromobutanes: A = 1-bromobutane, B = 2-bromobutane and C = 2-bromo-2-methylpropane. She warms each separately (a) with aqueous ethanol and (b) with NaI in dry acetone, and notes how fast each reacts.\n(i) Which isomer reacts fastest in (a), and why?\n(ii) Which isomer reacts fastest in (b), and why?\n(iii) Write the major product when B is heated with alcoholic KOH and name the rule that predicts it.", a: "(i) C. Aqueous ethanol favours SN1, and C forms the most stable tertiary carbocation, (CH₃)₃C⁺.\n(ii) A. NaI in dry acetone follows SN2, and the primary carbon of A is the least hindered for backside attack.\n(iii) But-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, is the major product (more substituted, more stable alkene), according to Saytzeff's rule.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "alcohols-phenols-and-ethers": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Phenol, when treated with CHCl₃ and aqueous NaOH followed by acidification, gives mainly:", options: ["salicylaldehyde", "salicylic acid", "benzaldehyde", "2,4,6-trichlorophenol"], answer: 0, explain: "Reimer–Tiemann reaction: the electrophile :CCl₂ (dichlorocarbene) attacks the ortho position of the phenoxide ion, and hydrolysis gives 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Lucas reagent is a mixture of:", options: ["conc. H₂SO₄ and NaCl", "dilute HCl and anhydrous ZnCl₂", "conc. HCl and anhydrous ZnCl₂", "aqueous HCl and zinc dust"], answer: 2, explain: "Lucas reagent = conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂. It is used to distinguish 1°, 2° and 3° alcohols by the speed with which turbidity (alkyl chloride) appears.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Williamson synthesis of ethers involves the reaction of:", options: ["two molecules of alkyl halide with sodium", "an alkyl halide with a sodium alkoxide", "an alcohol with PCl₅", "an alkene with water in the presence of acid"], answer: 1, explain: "R–X + R′–ONa → R–O–R′ + NaX, an SN2 reaction of the alkoxide ion with a (preferably primary) alkyl halide.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The common name of methoxybenzene, C₆H₅OCH₃, is:", options: ["phenetole", "phenyl acetate", "benzyl methyl ether", "anisole"], answer: 3, explain: "C₆H₅OCH₃ is anisole; phenetole is C₆H₅OCH₂CH₃.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Heating ethanol with excess conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K gives mainly:", options: ["ethene", "ethoxyethane", "ethanal", "ethane"], answer: 0, explain: "At 443 K intramolecular dehydration gives CH₂=CH₂. At 413 K with excess ethanol, intermolecular dehydration gives ethoxyethane.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The correct order of acid strength is:", options: ["ethanol > phenol > p-nitrophenol", "p-nitrophenol > phenol > ethanol", "phenol > p-nitrophenol > ethanol", "p-nitrophenol > ethanol > phenol"], answer: 1, explain: "Phenoxide is resonance-stabilised, so phenol is far more acidic than ethanol. The –NO₂ group in the para position withdraws electrons and stabilises the phenoxide further, so p-nitrophenol is the strongest.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Hydroboration–oxidation of propene (B₂H₆, then H₂O₂/OH⁻) gives:", options: ["propan-2-ol", "propanal", "propan-1-ol", "propane-1,2-diol"], answer: 2, explain: "The net result is anti-Markovnikov addition of water: boron adds to the less substituted carbon, so oxidation gives CH₃CH₂CH₂OH.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In the cumene process, phenol is obtained along with:", options: ["benzoic acid", "propanone", "ethanal", "methanol"], answer: 1, explain: "Cumene is oxidised by air to cumene hydroperoxide; treatment with dilute acid gives C₆H₅OH and CH₃COCH₃ (propanone, acetone).", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Ethanol is a weaker acid than water.\nReason (R): The electron-releasing ethyl group stabilises the ethoxide ion.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true (pKa of ethanol about 15.9; water about 15.7). The +I effect of the ethyl group destabilises the ethoxide ion by increasing electron density on oxygen, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Dilute nitric acid at 298 K nitrates phenol to give a mixture of o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol. A chemist must separate the two. The best method and reason is:", options: ["filtration, because the p-isomer is insoluble in the mixture", "steam distillation; the p-isomer distils over because of intramolecular H-bonding", "treatment with aqueous NaOH, which dissolves only the ortho isomer", "steam distillation; the o-isomer distils over because of intramolecular H-bonding"], answer: 3, explain: "In o-nitrophenol the –OH forms an intramolecular H-bond with the adjacent –NO₂, so the molecules are not associated and the isomer is steam-volatile. p-Nitrophenol is intermolecularly H-bonded and stays behind.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ is not prepared in good yield from (CH₃)₃C–Br and CH₃ONa.\nReason (R): Tertiary alkyl halides undergo elimination in the presence of strongly basic alkoxides.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "CH₃O⁻ is a strong base and (CH₃)₃CBr is tertiary, so E2 dominates and 2-methylpropene forms instead of the ether. The ether is made from (CH₃)₃CONa + CH₃Br.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Cleavage of (CH₃)₃C–O–CH₃ with HI gives mainly:", options: ["(CH₃)₃COH + CH₃I", "(CH₃)₃CI + CH₃I", "(CH₃)₂C=CH₂ + CH₃OH", "(CH₃)₃CI + CH₃OH"], answer: 3, explain: "With a tertiary alkyl group the protonated ether cleaves by SN1 because the tertiary carbocation is stable, so I⁻ combines with it: (CH₃)₃C–I + CH₃OH. Cleavage with only primary or secondary groups is SN2 at the smaller group.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A compound C₄H₁₀O gives turbidity immediately with Lucas reagent and is not oxidised by acidified K₂Cr₂O₇. It is:", options: ["2-methylpropan-2-ol", "butan-2-ol", "butan-1-ol", "2-methylpropan-1-ol"], answer: 0, explain: "Immediate turbidity means a tertiary alcohol (stable carbocation). A tertiary alcohol also resists oxidation because it has no hydrogen on the carbinol carbon. The only tertiary C₄H₁₀O alcohol is (CH₃)₃COH.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: In the cumene process, cumene (C₆H₅CH(CH₃)₂, M = 120 g mol⁻¹) is oxidised by air and then treated with dilute acid to give phenol (M = 94 g mol⁻¹) and propanone. A plant starts with 60 kg of cumene and the overall yield of phenol is 80%. The mass of phenol obtained is:", options: ["47.0 kg", "37.6 kg", "30.1 kg", "75.2 kg"], answer: 1, explain: "60 kg = 60 000 g ÷ 120 = 500 mol of cumene → 500 mol phenol (1:1) = 500 × 94 = 47 000 g = 47.0 kg at 100%. At 80% yield: 0.80 × 47.0 = 37.6 kg.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the IUPAC name of (CH₃)₂CHCH₂OH.", a: "2-Methylpropan-1-ol.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What products are formed when anisole is heated with HI? Why is iodobenzene not formed?", a: "Phenol and iodomethane (C₆H₅OCH₃ + HI → C₆H₅OH + CH₃I). The C(aryl)–O bond has partial double-bond character because of resonance, so it is stronger and is not cleaved.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Explain why (i) phenol is more acidic than ethanol, and (ii) the C–O bond in phenol is shorter than in methanol.", a: "(i) Phenoxide ion is stabilised by resonance, the negative charge being delocalised over the ring (ortho and para carbons), whereas ethoxide is destabilised by the +I effect of the ethyl group. So phenol loses H⁺ more readily (pKa ≈ 10 against ≈ 15.9).\n(ii) In phenol the oxygen lone pair is conjugated with the ring, giving the C–O bond partial double-bond character, and the carbon is sp² hybridised (more s-character), so the bond is shorter (136 pm) than in methanol (sp³ carbon, 142 pm).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How will you distinguish propan-1-ol, propan-2-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol using Lucas reagent?", a: "Shake each with conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂ at room temperature.\n2-Methylpropan-2-ol (3°) gives turbidity (alkyl chloride) immediately; propan-2-ol (2°) gives turbidity after about 5 minutes; propan-1-ol (1°) gives no turbidity at room temperature (only on heating).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write the equations (with conditions) for: (i) Reimer–Tiemann reaction of phenol, (ii) Kolbe's reaction of sodium phenoxide, (iii) Williamson synthesis of methoxyethane.", a: "(i) C₆H₅OH + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH (343–353 K), then H⁺ → 2-HOC₆H₄CHO (salicylaldehyde).\n(ii) C₆H₅ONa + CO₂ (400 K, 4–7 atm), then H⁺ → 2-HOC₆H₄COOH (salicylic acid).\n(iii) CH₃CH₂Br + CH₃ONa → CH₃OCH₂CH₃ + NaBr (SN2).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is tert-butyl methyl ether prepared from sodium tert-butoxide and bromomethane and not from tert-butyl bromide and sodium methoxide? Write both reactions.", a: "Williamson synthesis is SN2, which needs an unhindered (methyl or primary) alkyl halide. A tertiary halide reacts with a strong base (alkoxide) by elimination.\n(CH₃)₃CONa + CH₃Br → (CH₃)₃COCH₃ + NaBr (good yield).\n(CH₃)₃CBr + CH₃ONa → (CH₃)₂C=CH₂ + CH₃OH + NaBr (alkene formed instead of ether).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the mechanism of the acid-catalysed dehydration of ethanol to ethene at 443 K, showing the three steps.", a: "Step 1: Protonation of the alcohol: CH₃CH₂–OH + H⁺ → CH₃CH₂–OH₂⁺ (ethyl oxonium ion).\nStep 2: Loss of water to form the carbocation (slow, rate-determining): CH₃CH₂–OH₂⁺ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O.\nStep 3: Loss of a proton from the carbon next to the cation: CH₃CH₂⁺ → CH₂=CH₂ + H⁺ (the acid is regenerated).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the reagents for each conversion: (i) propene to propan-1-ol, (ii) methanal to ethanol, (iii) phenol to 2,4,6-tribromophenol, (iv) benzene to phenol (via sulphonation), (v) ethanol to ethoxyethane.", a: "(i) B₂H₆ (diborane) in THF, then H₂O₂/OH⁻ (anti-Markovnikov hydration).\n(ii) CH₃MgBr in dry ether, then H₂O/H⁺: HCHO → CH₃CH₂OMgBr → CH₃CH₂OH.\n(iii) Bromine water (excess): white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol.\n(iv) Conc. H₂SO₄ (oleum) → C₆H₅SO₃H; fuse with NaOH → C₆H₅ONa; acidify with H⁺ → C₆H₅OH.\n(v) 2CH₃CH₂OH → CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃ + H₂O with conc. H₂SO₄ at 413 K (excess ethanol).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Arrange p-nitrophenol, phenol, p-cresol, water and ethanol in decreasing order of acid strength and give reasons. State one test that shows phenol is more acidic than ethanol.", a: "Order: p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol > water > ethanol (pKa about 7.1, 10, 10.2, 15.7, 15.9).\n(1) Phenols are more acidic than water and alcohols because the phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilised, with charge spread over oxygen and the ring.\n(2) The –NO₂ group at the para position is electron-withdrawing (−I, −R), which stabilises the phenoxide further and increases acidity.\n(3) The –CH₃ group of p-cresol is electron-releasing (+I), which destabilises the phenoxide and decreases acidity relative to phenol.\n(4) Alkyl groups raise electron density on oxygen of alkoxides, so alcohols are weaker acids than water (and 1° > 2° > 3° in acidity).\nTest: phenol dissolves in aqueous NaOH (forming sodium phenoxide) while ethanol does not; or phenol reacts with Na/NaOH and gives a violet colour with neutral FeCl₃, which ethanol does not.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is made from phenol in two stages. First, sodium phenoxide is heated with CO₂ at 400 K and 4–7 atm and then acidified to give salicylic acid. Second, salicylic acid is acetylated with ethanoic anhydride.\n(i) Name the reaction in the first stage and the product formed on acidification.\n(ii) Why is phenol first converted to sodium phenoxide?\n(iii) How would you test an aspirin sample for contamination by unreacted salicylic acid?", a: "(i) Kolbe's reaction; sodium salicylate is formed and acidification gives salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid).\n(ii) The phenoxide ion is much more electron-rich (more strongly activated) than phenol, so the ring can be attacked by the weak electrophile CO₂.\n(iii) Add a few drops of neutral FeCl₃ solution to the sample. Salicylic acid has a free phenolic –OH and gives a violet colour; pure aspirin (phenolic –OH acetylated) gives no colour.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "aldehydes-ketones-and-carboxylic-acids": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The reduction of an acyl chloride to an aldehyde with H₂ over Pd supported on BaSO₄ is called:", options: ["Stephen reaction", "Rosenmund reduction", "Etard reaction", "Clemmensen reduction"], answer: 1, explain: "Rosenmund reduction: RCOCl + H₂ → RCHO + HCl (Pd–BaSO₄, with a poison such as quinoline or sulphur to prevent further reduction to the alcohol).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following undergoes the Cannizzaro reaction?", options: ["Ethanal", "Propanone", "Propanal", "Methanal"], answer: 3, explain: "The Cannizzaro reaction needs an aldehyde with no α-hydrogen, such as HCHO or C₆H₅CHO. Ethanal and propanal have α-hydrogens and give aldol products instead.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which compound gives a silver mirror with Tollens' reagent?", options: ["Ethanal", "Propanone", "Cyclohexanone", "Acetophenone"], answer: 0, explain: "Aldehydes are oxidised by [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ to carboxylate ions, depositing metallic silver. Ketones do not respond.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CHO is:", options: ["2-ethylpropanal", "pentanal", "2-methylbutanal", "3-methylbutanal"], answer: 2, explain: "The longest chain containing –CHO has 4 carbons (butanal), numbering from the CHO carbon; the methyl group is on C-2.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Carboxylic acids react with sodium hydrogencarbonate to release:", options: ["H₂", "O₂", "CO₂", "CO"], answer: 2, explain: "RCOOH + NaHCO₃ → RCOONa + H₂O + CO₂↑. This brisk effervescence is a test for –COOH, since phenols are too weak to do this.", level: 1 },

      { q: "When ethanal is treated with dilute NaOH and the aldol is then heated, the final product is:", options: ["3-hydroxybutanal", "but-3-enal", "but-2-enal", "butan-2-one"], answer: 2, explain: "Ethanal + dil. NaOH → CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO (aldol). On heating it loses water: CH₃CH=CHCHO (but-2-enal), an α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following represents the correct order of acid strength?", options: ["CH₃COOH > ClCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH", "FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH", "ClCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH", "CH₃COOH > FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH"], answer: 1, explain: "Electron-withdrawing halogens stabilise the carboxylate ion by the −I effect. Fluorine is more electronegative than chlorine, so FCH₂COOH is stronger than ClCH₂COOH, which is stronger than CH₃COOH.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A ketone sensitive to acid but stable to strong base needs its C=O group converted to CH₂. The best method is:", options: ["NaBH₄ in ethanol", "Clemmensen reduction", "Rosenmund reduction", "Wolff–Kishner reduction"], answer: 3, explain: "Wolff–Kishner (NH₂NH₂ then KOH in ethylene glycol, heat) works in strongly basic medium. Clemmensen (Zn–Hg, conc. HCl) is acidic. NaBH₄ stops at the alcohol.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Benzaldehyde does not undergo aldol condensation.\nReason (R): Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen atom.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Aldol condensation needs an α-hydrogen to form the enolate ion. In C₆H₅CHO the carbon next to –CHO is a ring carbon with no hydrogen that can be removed this way, so R correctly explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A forensic student has two liquids, X and Y, each of molecular formula C₃H₆O. X gives a silver mirror with Tollens' reagent. Y does not, but it gives a yellow precipitate on warming with I₂ and NaOH. X and Y are respectively:", options: ["propanone and propanal", "propanal and propanone", "prop-2-en-1-ol and propanone", "propanal and prop-2-en-1-ol"], answer: 1, explain: "A silver mirror shows an aldehyde: CH₃CH₂CHO (propanal). The iodoform test shows a CH₃CO– group: CH₃COCH₃ (propanone).", level: 2 },

      { q: "A compound C₈H₈O gives an orange precipitate with 2,4-DNP, a yellow precipitate with I₂/NaOH, and no silver mirror with Tollens' reagent. It is:", options: ["1-phenylethanone", "phenylethanal", "p-methylbenzaldehyde", "styrene oxide"], answer: 0, explain: "2,4-DNP shows C=O; no Tollens' result means a ketone; the iodoform test needs CH₃CO–. So C₆H₅COCH₃ (acetophenone). The two isomeric aldehydes would give silver mirrors.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In the crossed Cannizzaro reaction of benzaldehyde with an excess of methanal in conc. NaOH, the main products are:", options: ["benzoic acid and methanol", "sodium benzoate and methanol", "benzyl alcohol and sodium methanoate", "benzyl alcohol and sodium benzoate"], answer: 2, explain: "Methanal is the more reactive aldehyde (more easily attacked by OH⁻ and a better hydride donor), so it is oxidised to HCOONa while benzaldehyde is reduced to C₆H₅CH₂OH.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Methanoic acid is a stronger acid than ethanoic acid.\nReason (R): Methanoic acid reduces Tollens' reagent.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "A is true (pKa 3.75 against 4.76) because the CH₃ group of ethanoic acid releases electrons and destabilises the carboxylate. R is also true (HCOOH has an aldehydic –CHO character), but it has nothing to do with acid strength.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A food inspector titrates a vinegar sample (ethanoic acid, M = 60 g mol⁻¹). A 10.0 mL portion needs 25.0 mL of 0.20 M NaOH for complete neutralisation. The mass/volume percentage of ethanoic acid in the vinegar is:", options: ["6.0%", "0.30%", "30%", "3.0%"], answer: 3, explain: "Moles NaOH = 0.025 × 0.20 = 0.0050 mol = moles of CH₃COOH (1:1) in 10.0 mL. Mass = 0.0050 × 60 = 0.30 g per 10 mL = 3.0 g per 100 mL, so 3.0% w/v.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the equation for the Rosenmund reduction of benzoyl chloride.", a: "C₆H₅COCl + H₂ → C₆H₅CHO + HCl (catalyst Pd on BaSO₄).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the reagent used in the Etard reaction to convert toluene into benzaldehyde.", a: "Chromyl chloride (CrO₂Cl₂) in CS₂ gives a chromium complex, which is hydrolysed with H₃O⁺ to C₆H₅CHO.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How can pentan-2-one and pentan-3-one be distinguished chemically? Write the equation for the positive test.", a: "Warm each with I₂ and NaOH (iodoform test). Pentan-2-one contains the CH₃CO– group and gives a yellow precipitate of iodoform; pentan-3-one does not.\nCH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃ + 3NaOI → CHI₃↓ + CH₃CH₂CH₂COONa + 2NaOH.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Carboxylic acids are stronger acids than phenols, though both give resonance-stabilised anions. Explain.", a: "In the carboxylate ion the negative charge is shared equally by two electronegative oxygen atoms (two equivalent resonance structures).\nIn the phenoxide ion the charge is delocalised partly onto less electronegative carbon atoms of the ring (non-equivalent structures), so it is less stable. Hence RCOOH loses H⁺ more easily (and, for example, liberates CO₂ from NaHCO₃ while phenol does not).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write the equation (with reagents) for: (i) Clemmensen reduction of acetophenone, (ii) Wolff–Kishner reduction of propanone, (iii) Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction of propanoic acid with bromine.", a: "(i) C₆H₅COCH₃ → C₆H₅CH₂CH₃ + H₂O (Zn–Hg, conc. HCl).\n(ii) CH₃COCH₃ + NH₂NH₂ → (CH₃)₂C=NNH₂ (hydrazone) + H₂O; then KOH, ethylene glycol, heat → CH₃CH₂CH₃ + N₂.\n(iii) CH₃CH₂COOH + Br₂ → CH₃CHBrCOOH + HBr (red phosphorus catalyst; gives 2-bromopropanoic acid).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the products of: (i) benzaldehyde with conc. NaOH (Cannizzaro), (ii) ethanal with dilute NaOH, followed by heating, (iii) Explain why benzaldehyde cannot undergo the aldol reaction.", a: "(i) 2C₆H₅CHO + NaOH (conc.) → C₆H₅CH₂OH (benzyl alcohol) + C₆H₅COONa (sodium benzoate).\n(ii) 2CH₃CHO → CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO (3-hydroxybutanal, aldol); on heating this loses H₂O to give CH₃CH=CHCHO (but-2-enal).\n(iii) Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen, so it cannot form the enolate ion required for the aldol reaction.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the reagents and the aldehyde product for: (i) Stephen reaction of ethanenitrile, (ii) Etard reaction of toluene, (iii) Gattermann–Koch reaction of benzene.", a: "(i) CH₃CN + SnCl₂/HCl → CH₃CH=NH·HCl; hydrolysis with H₃O⁺ gives CH₃CHO.\n(ii) C₆H₅CH₃ + CrO₂Cl₂ (CS₂) → brown chromium complex; H₃O⁺ hydrolysis gives C₆H₅CHO.\n(iii) C₆H₆ + CO + HCl (anhydrous AlCl₃/CuCl) → C₆H₅CHO.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Give one chemical test to distinguish between: (i) propanal and propanone, (ii) ethanal and benzaldehyde, (iii) benzoic acid and phenol, (iv) acetophenone and benzophenone, (v) methanoic acid and ethanoic acid.", a: "(i) Tollens' test: propanal gives a silver mirror; propanone does not.\n(ii) Fehling's test: ethanal gives a red-brown precipitate of Cu₂O; benzaldehyde does not.\n(iii) NaHCO₃: benzoic acid gives brisk effervescence of CO₂; phenol does not (phenol gives a violet colour with neutral FeCl₃).\n(iv) Iodoform test: acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃) gives yellow CHI₃; benzophenone does not.\n(v) Tollens' (or Fehling's) test: methanoic acid gives a silver mirror; ethanoic acid does not.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "An organic compound A (C₄H₈O) gives an orange precipitate with 2,4-DNP reagent, does not reduce Tollens' reagent and gives a yellow precipitate with I₂/NaOH. Identify A and write the equations for its reactions with (i) 2,4-DNP, (ii) NaBH₄, (iii) Zn–Hg/conc. HCl and (iv) I₂/NaOH. Give the reasoning.", a: "Reasoning: 2,4-DNP positive means a carbonyl group; Tollens' negative means a ketone; iodoform positive means CH₃CO–. The only C₄H₈O ketone is A = CH₃COCH₂CH₃ (butan-2-one).\n(i) CH₃COCH₂CH₃ + 2,4-DNP → CH₃C(=N–NH–C₆H₃(NO₂)₂)CH₂CH₃ + H₂O (orange precipitate).\n(ii) NaBH₄ gives CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (butan-2-ol).\n(iii) Clemmensen gives CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ (butane).\n(iv) CH₃COCH₂CH₃ + 3NaOI → CHI₃↓ (yellow) + CH₃CH₂COONa + 2NaOH.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Formalin, a 40% aqueous solution of methanal, is used to preserve biological specimens. Methanal is a gas at room temperature, has no α-hydrogen, and is a strong reducing agent.\n(i) Write the Cannizzaro reaction of methanal with conc. NaOH.\n(ii) Why does methanal give the Cannizzaro reaction but ethanal does not?\n(iii) Write the equation for the reaction of methanal with Tollens' reagent.", a: "(i) 2HCHO + NaOH (conc.) → CH₃OH + HCOONa.\n(ii) Methanal has no α-hydrogen, so OH⁻ attacks the carbonyl carbon and a hydride is transferred to a second molecule. Ethanal has α-hydrogens, and with dilute alkali OH⁻ removes one to give the enolate, leading to aldol condensation.\n(iii) HCHO + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + 3OH⁻ → HCOO⁻ + 2Ag↓ + 4NH₃ + 2H₂O (silver mirror).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "amines": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The carbylamine test (CHCl₃ with alcoholic KOH, heat) is positive for:", options: ["secondary amines", "tertiary amines", "primary amines", "quaternary ammonium salts"], answer: 2, explain: "Only primary amines (aliphatic or aromatic) give the foul-smelling isocyanide: RNH₂ + CHCl₃ + 3KOH → RNC + 3KCl + 3H₂O.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Hoffmann bromamide degradation of ethanamide (CH₃CONH₂) gives:", options: ["ethanamine", "methanamine", "dimethylamine", "aniline"], answer: 1, explain: "RCONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → RNH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O. The amine has one carbon fewer than the amide, so CH₃CONH₂ gives CH₃NH₂.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Hinsberg's reagent is:", options: ["benzoyl chloride", "benzenesulphonic acid", "benzenediazonium chloride", "benzenesulphonyl chloride"], answer: 3, explain: "Hinsberg's reagent, C₆H₅SO₂Cl, is used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary amines.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Aniline treated with NaNO₂ and HCl at 273–278 K gives:", options: ["nitrobenzene", "phenol", "benzenediazonium chloride", "azobenzene"], answer: 2, explain: "Diazotisation: C₆H₅NH₂ + NaNO₂ + 2HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + NaCl + 2H₂O. Above about 283 K the salt decomposes to phenol.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a secondary amine?", options: ["(CH₃)₂NH", "(CH₃)₃N", "CH₃CH₂NH₂", "C₆H₅NH₂"], answer: 0, explain: "A secondary amine has two carbon groups on nitrogen (R₂NH): dimethylamine. (CH₃)₃N is tertiary; the other two are primary.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The correct order of basic strength of methyl-substituted amines in aqueous solution is:", options: ["(CH₃)₃N > (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > NH₃", "(CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N > NH₃", "CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₂NH > (CH₃)₃N > NH₃", "NH₃ > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₂NH > (CH₃)₃N"], answer: 1, explain: "In water the basicity depends on the +I effect, solvation of the ammonium ion by H-bonding and steric hindrance. For methyl amines the secondary amine is best balanced, and the tertiary amine is weakened by poor solvation.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Benzamide on Hoffmann bromamide degradation gives:", options: ["benzylamine", "benzoic acid", "aniline", "benzonitrile"], answer: 2, explain: "C₆H₅CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → C₆H₅NH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O. The carbonyl carbon is lost as carbonate, so the product has one carbon less and the –NH₂ is attached directly to the ring.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following amines cannot be prepared by the Gabriel phthalimide synthesis?", options: ["Aniline", "Ethanamine", "Propan-1-amine", "Phenylmethanamine"], answer: 0, explain: "Gabriel synthesis needs the phthalimide anion to attack an alkyl halide by SN2. Aryl halides do not undergo this substitution, so aromatic amines like aniline cannot be made. Benzylamine can be made from benzyl chloride.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Aniline is a weaker base than ammonia.\nReason (R): The lone pair on nitrogen in aniline is delocalised into the benzene ring.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Delocalisation (resonance) makes the lone pair less available for protonation, and the anilinium ion is less stabilised. pKb: aniline 9.38 against 4.75 for NH₃.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A dye chemist prepares benzenediazonium chloride at 273–278 K and adds it to an alkaline solution of phenol. An orange dye forms. The dye is:", options: ["azoxybenzene", "p-aminoazobenzene", "diphenylamine", "p-hydroxyazobenzene"], answer: 3, explain: "Diazonium coupling: the diazonium ion attacks the para position of the phenoxide ion, giving HO–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅ (p-hydroxyazobenzene, orange). With aniline the product would be p-aminoazobenzene (yellow).", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): p-Toluidine is a stronger base than aniline.\nReason (R): The –CH₃ group is electron-withdrawing and stabilises the anilinium ion.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: the electron-releasing –CH₃ group (+I and hyperconjugation) increases electron density on nitrogen. R is false because –CH₃ is electron-releasing, not electron-withdrawing.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The correct order of basic strength of aniline, p-toluidine and p-nitroaniline is:", options: ["p-nitroaniline > aniline > p-toluidine", "p-toluidine > aniline > p-nitroaniline", "aniline > p-toluidine > p-nitroaniline", "p-toluidine > p-nitroaniline > aniline"], answer: 1, explain: "Electron-releasing –CH₃ increases availability of the lone pair, while the electron-withdrawing –NO₂ (−I, −R) greatly reduces it. So p-toluidine > aniline > p-nitroaniline.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Nitrobenzene is reduced with Sn/HCl to A, A is diazotised at 273–278 K to B, B is treated with CuCN/KCN to give C, and C is heated with aqueous acid to give D. D is:", options: ["benzamide", "benzylamine", "phenol", "benzoic acid"], answer: 3, explain: "A = aniline; B = benzenediazonium chloride; C = benzonitrile (Sandmeyer cyanation); D = benzoic acid (hydrolysis of –CN with H₃O⁺, heat, passing through benzamide).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A student has three unlabelled amines: ethylamine, diethylamine and triethylamine. Each is shaken with benzenesulphonyl chloride in aqueous KOH. Sample X gives a clear solution, Y gives an insoluble product, and Z does not react. X, Y and Z are respectively:", options: ["ethylamine, diethylamine, triethylamine", "diethylamine, ethylamine, triethylamine", "triethylamine, diethylamine, ethylamine", "ethylamine, triethylamine, diethylamine"], answer: 0, explain: "A primary amine gives a sulphonamide with an acidic N–H that dissolves in alkali (X = ethylamine). A secondary amine's sulphonamide has no N–H, so it stays insoluble (Y = diethylamine). A tertiary amine does not react (Z = triethylamine).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the IUPAC name of (CH₃CH₂)₂NCH₃.", a: "N-Ethyl-N-methylethanamine.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why can aromatic primary amines not be prepared by the Gabriel phthalimide synthesis?", a: "Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion (the C–X bond is strong because of resonance and the carbon is sp² hybridised), so the N-arylphthalimide cannot form.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is aniline (pKb = 9.38) a much weaker base than methylamine (pKb = 3.38)?", a: "In aniline the nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance, so it is less available to accept a proton, and the anilinium ion has no such stabilisation.\nIn methylamine the +I effect of CH₃ increases electron density on nitrogen, making it more basic than ammonia.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How will you distinguish aniline from N-methylaniline? Write the equation for the positive test.", a: "Carbylamine test: heat each with CHCl₃ and alcoholic KOH. Aniline (primary) gives the foul smell of phenyl isocyanide; N-methylaniline (secondary) does not.\nC₆H₅NH₂ + CHCl₃ + 3KOH → C₆H₅NC + 3KCl + 3H₂O.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the Gabriel phthalimide synthesis of ethanamine. Write the steps.", a: "(1) Phthalimide + KOH → potassium phthalimide (the acidic N–H is removed).\n(2) Potassium phthalimide + CH₃CH₂Br → N-ethylphthalimide + KBr (SN2).\n(3) Alkaline hydrolysis (aq. NaOH, heat) → CH₃CH₂NH₂ (ethanamine) + sodium phthalate (disodium benzene-1,2-dicarboxylate). It gives pure primary amines without 2° or 3° amine by-products.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the equation for the Hoffmann bromamide degradation of propanamide. Explain why the amine has one carbon atom less.", a: "CH₃CH₂CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O.\nThe amide is N-brominated, then rearranges to the isocyanate R–N=C=O (the alkyl group migrates from C to N). The isocyanate is hydrolysed by NaOH to R–NH₂ and carbonate, so the carbonyl carbon is lost as CO₃²⁻ and the amine has one carbon fewer (ethanamine, C₂).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the product, with the name of the reaction and reagent, when benzenediazonium chloride is treated with (i) CuCl/HCl, (ii) KI, (iii) H₃PO₂ and water.", a: "(i) Sandmeyer reaction: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + CuCl/HCl → C₆H₅Cl (chlorobenzene) + N₂.\n(ii) C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + KI → C₆H₅I (iodobenzene) + N₂ + KCl.\n(iii) C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₃PO₂ + H₂O → C₆H₆ (benzene) + N₂ + H₃PO₃ + HCl.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the reagents and equations for the conversions: (i) benzene to aniline, (ii) aniline to fluorobenzene, (iii) ethanenitrile to ethanamine, (iv) bromoethane to propan-1-amine, (v) aniline to phenol.", a: "(i) C₆H₆ → C₆H₅NO₂ (conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄, 330 K) → C₆H₅NH₂ (Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, then NaOH).\n(ii) C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (NaNO₂/HCl, 273–278 K) → C₆H₅N₂⁺BF₄⁻ (HBF₄) → C₆H₅F + N₂ + BF₃ (heat).\n(iii) CH₃CN + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂NH₂ (LiAlH₄ or H₂/Ni).\n(iv) CH₃CH₂Br + KCN → CH₃CH₂CN, then LiAlH₄ (or H₂/Ni) → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂.\n(v) C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (NaNO₂/HCl, 273–278 K), then warm with water → C₆H₅OH + N₂ + HCl.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Arrange ammonia, ethanamine, diethylamine, triethylamine and aniline in decreasing order of basic strength in aqueous solution. Explain the order using the factors involved.", a: "Order: (C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃ > C₆H₅NH₂.\n(1) +I effect: alkyl groups push electron density to nitrogen, so all the ethylamines are stronger bases than NH₃.\n(2) Solvation: the ammonium ion formed is stabilised by H-bonding with water; this is greatest for R–NH₃⁺ (three N–H), least for R₃NH⁺ (one N–H).\n(3) Steric hindrance: bulky alkyl groups hinder protonation and solvation, which weakens the tertiary amine.\n(4) The balance of these gives the secondary amine > tertiary > primary > NH₃.\n(5) Aniline is the weakest (pKb 9.38 against 4.75 for NH₃) because its lone pair is delocalised into the ring.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A chemist converts aniline into an azo dye in two steps. In step 1, aniline is treated with NaNO₂ and HCl at 273–278 K. In step 2, the product is added to an alkaline solution of phenol, and an orange dye separates.\n(i) Write the equation for step 1 and say why the temperature is kept low.\n(ii) Name the reaction in step 2 and give the structure of the dye.\n(iii) Why are aromatic diazonium salts more stable than aliphatic ones?", a: "(i) C₆H₅NH₂ + NaNO₂ + 2HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + NaCl + 2H₂O. The temperature is kept low because benzenediazonium chloride decomposes above about 283 K, giving phenol and N₂.\n(ii) Diazonium coupling. The diazonium ion attacks the para position of the phenoxide ion to give p-hydroxyazobenzene, HO–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅ (orange dye).\n(iii) In arenediazonium ions the positive charge on the N₂⁺ group is delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance; aliphatic diazonium ions have no such stabilisation and decompose at once.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "biomolecules": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Hydrolysis of sucrose with dilute acid gives:", options: ["glucose and galactose", "glucose and fructose", "two molecules of glucose", "glucose and mannose"], answer: 1, explain: "C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) + C₆H₁₂O₆ (fructose). Maltose gives two glucose and lactose gives galactose + glucose.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Scurvy is caused by the deficiency of vitamin:", options: ["A", "D", "C", "K"], answer: 2, explain: "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) deficiency causes scurvy, with bleeding gums. A causes night blindness, D causes rickets, K delays blood clotting.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Glucose forms a pentaacetate with acetic anhydride. This shows that glucose has:", options: ["five –OH groups", "six –OH groups", "one ketone group", "five aldehyde groups"], answer: 0, explain: "Each –OH group is acetylated, so five acetyl groups mean five hydroxyl groups (one on each of five carbons).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The linkage that joins amino acids in a protein is:", options: ["–CO–O–", "–C–O–C–", "–NH–NH–", "–CO–NH–"], answer: 3, explain: "A peptide bond is an amide link formed between the –COOH of one amino acid and the –NH₂ of the next, with loss of water.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a DNA double helix, guanine pairs with:", options: ["adenine", "cytosine", "thymine", "uracil"], answer: 1, explain: "G pairs with C through three hydrogen bonds; A pairs with T through two. Uracil occurs in RNA, not DNA.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Hydrolysis of lactose gives:", options: ["glucose and fructose", "two molecules of glucose", "galactose and glucose", "galactose and fructose"], answer: 2, explain: "Lactose (milk sugar) = β-D-galactose joined to β-D-glucose by a C1–C4 glycosidic link.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In amylopectin, the branches arise through glycosidic links between:", options: ["C1 and C4 of β-D-glucose", "C1 and C2 of glucose", "C1 and C6 of α-D-glucose", "C1 and C3 of glucose"], answer: 2, explain: "The main chains of amylopectin have C1–C4 α-links, and the branch points have C1–C6 α-links. Amylose is unbranched.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Glycine in aqueous solution exists mainly as:", options: ["H₂NCH₂COOH", "H₃N⁺CH₂COO⁻", "H₃N⁺CH₂COOH", "H₂NCH₂COO⁻"], answer: 1, explain: "The acidic –COOH transfers a proton to the basic –NH₂ within the same molecule, giving the dipolar ion (zwitterion) H₃N⁺CH₂COO⁻. This explains the high melting point and solubility in water.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.\nReason (R): In sucrose the reducing groups of glucose (C1) and fructose (C2) are both involved in the glycosidic linkage.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "With no free hemiacetal or hemiketal group, sucrose cannot open to give a free –CHO or –COCH₂OH, so it does not reduce Fehling's or Tollens' reagent.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A student warms sucrose solution with Fehling's reagent and sees no change. She then boils sucrose with a few drops of dilute HCl, neutralises it, and warms it with Fehling's reagent again: a red-brown precipitate forms. This is because:", options: ["HCl reduces Cu²⁺ ions to Cu₂O", "sucrose is reducing only after it is heated", "sucrose polymerises into starch in acid", "hydrolysis gives glucose and fructose, which are reducing sugars"], answer: 3, explain: "Sucrose is non-reducing, but acid hydrolysis frees glucose and fructose (invert sugar), whose free carbonyl groups reduce Cu²⁺ to red Cu₂O.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Denaturation of a protein destroys its biological activity.\nReason (R): Denaturation breaks the peptide bonds and so alters the primary structure of the protein.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: the shape needed for activity is lost. R is false: denaturation (heat, pH change) disturbs hydrogen bonds and other weak interactions, destroying the secondary and tertiary structure, but the peptide bonds and the primary structure remain intact.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Open-chain D-glucose has how many chiral carbon atoms, and how many stereoisomers does this allow?", options: ["5 chiral carbons; 32 stereoisomers", "4 chiral carbons; 16 stereoisomers", "3 chiral carbons; 8 stereoisomers", "6 chiral carbons; 64 stereoisomers"], answer: 1, explain: "In CHO–CHOH–CHOH–CHOH–CHOH–CH₂OH, C2 to C5 are chiral (4 centres), giving 2⁴ = 16 stereoisomers. The cyclic form has an extra chiral centre at C1 (the anomeric carbon).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A tripeptide is formed from three glycine molecules (M = 75 g mol⁻¹). Its molar mass is:", options: ["225 g mol⁻¹", "207 g mol⁻¹", "171 g mol⁻¹", "189 g mol⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "Forming a tripeptide creates two peptide bonds and releases two molecules of water: 3 × 75 − 2 × 18 = 225 − 36 = 189 g mol⁻¹.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A forensic laboratory analyses a double-stranded DNA sample and finds that 30% of its bases are adenine. The percentage of cytosine in the sample is:", options: ["20%", "30%", "40%", "70%"], answer: 0, explain: "A pairs with T, so T is also 30%; A + T = 60%. The remaining 40% is G + C, shared equally because G pairs with C, so C = 20%.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What type of bonds hold the two strands of a DNA double helix together?", a: "Hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: two between A and T and three between G and C.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the vitamin whose deficiency causes rickets and give one source of it.", a: "Vitamin D; sources include fish liver oil, egg yolk and exposure to sunlight (the body makes it on skin).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Glucose has an aldehyde group but does not give Schiff's test and does not form the hydrogensulphite addition product. Explain.", a: "Glucose exists mainly in the cyclic hemiacetal form, in which the C5–OH has added to the –CHO group. Only a negligible fraction is in the open-chain form that has a free –CHO group.\nSo the aldehydic reactions that need a free –CHO (Schiff's test, NaHSO₃ addition) are not shown.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between fibrous and globular proteins with one example of each.", a: "Fibrous proteins have polypeptide chains held side by side as fibres by hydrogen and disulphide bonds; they are insoluble in water, e.g. keratin or myosin.\nGlobular proteins have chains folded into a spherical shape; they are soluble in water, e.g. insulin or albumins.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give the products of complete hydrolysis of (i) sucrose, (ii) lactose, (iii) maltose. Which of these are reducing sugars?", a: "(i) Sucrose → α-D-glucose + β-D-fructose; non-reducing (both anomeric carbons are tied in the glycosidic link).\n(ii) Lactose → β-D-galactose + β-D-glucose; reducing (free anomeric –OH on the glucose unit).\n(iii) Maltose → two molecules of α-D-glucose; reducing (free anomeric –OH on one unit).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the primary, secondary and tertiary structures of proteins.", a: "Primary: the sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in the polypeptide chain.\nSecondary: regular local folding of the chain, either the α-helix (right-handed coil) or the β-pleated sheet, stabilised by hydrogen bonds between –C=O and N–H groups.\nTertiary: the overall three-dimensional folding of the chain into a compact shape (globular or fibrous), held by hydrogen bonds, disulphide bridges, ionic (electrostatic) and van der Waals forces.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the vitamin whose deficiency causes (i) night blindness, (ii) beriberi, (iii) scurvy. State whether each is fat-soluble or water-soluble.", a: "(i) Vitamin A causes night blindness (and xerophthalmia); fat-soluble.\n(ii) Vitamin B₁ (thiamine) causes beriberi; water-soluble.\n(iii) Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) causes scurvy; water-soluble, so it must be taken regularly because it is not stored.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Compare starch, cellulose and glycogen with respect to (i) the monomer and linkage, (ii) branching, (iii) solubility or digestibility, (iv) where each occurs and (v) its function.", a: "Starch: α-D-glucose joined by C1–C4 (amylose, 15–20%, unbranched, water-soluble) and by C1–C4 plus C1–C6 links (amylopectin, 80–85%, branched, insoluble). Occurs in plants (cereals, potatoes); the storage form of energy.\nCellulose: β-D-glucose joined by C1–C4 β-links; unbranched, long straight chains; insoluble and not digestible by humans (no enzyme for β-links). Occurs in plant cell walls; structural role.\nGlycogen: α-D-glucose with C1–C4 and C1–C6 links; highly branched (animal starch); stored in the liver and muscles of animals as the energy reserve.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Describe the double-helical structure of DNA. Include (i) the components of a nucleotide, (ii) how nucleotides are linked, (iii) the base pairing rule and the number of hydrogen bonds, (iv) the sugar and bases present, and (v) one function.", a: "(i) A nucleotide = pentose sugar (β-D-2-deoxyribose in DNA) + nitrogenous base + phosphate group; the sugar–base unit alone is a nucleoside.\n(ii) Nucleotides are joined by phosphodiester linkages between the 5′ carbon of one sugar and the 3′ carbon of the next, forming a polynucleotide strand.\n(iii) Two strands run in opposite directions and coil as a right-handed double helix (Watson and Crick). Bases pair as A with T (two hydrogen bonds) and G with C (three hydrogen bonds), so the strands are complementary.\n(iv) DNA bases are adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine; RNA has uracil in place of thymine.\n(v) DNA stores hereditary information and replicates itself when cells divide; the sequence of bases directs protein synthesis through RNA.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Lactose intolerance occurs in people whose bodies make too little of the enzyme lactase, so lactose in milk is not hydrolysed. Lactase is a globular protein. Fever above about 41 °C can reduce the activity of enzymes.\n(i) Name the two monosaccharides formed on hydrolysis of lactose and the type of linkage broken.\n(ii) Why is an enzyme such as lactase said to be specific?\n(iii) Why does a very high temperature destroy the activity of an enzyme?", a: "(i) β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose; the β-glycosidic linkage (C1 of galactose to C4 of glucose) is broken.\n(ii) The enzyme's active site has a particular shape that fits only its own substrate (lactase acts only on lactose, as maltase acts only on maltose).\n(iii) High temperature denatures the protein: hydrogen bonds and other weak interactions break, the secondary and tertiary structure unfolds, the active site loses its shape and the enzyme loses its catalytic activity.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
